FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:776-789 — the new `--compare` CLI branch and `compare_with_baseline` (tests/bench/mod.rs:703-740) have no test on either side; grep shows no test ever passes `--compare` to `run_cli`/`run_cli_with_path`, and `tests/bench_test.rs:778` only exercises `render_comparison` directly. The file-read, parse, per-row re-run, and exit-code behavior of the user-facing mode are entirely uncovered. — Add a CLI test that writes a temp baseline, runs `run_cli_with_path(&["--compare", path], ...)`, and asserts exit 0 plus the expected rows, mirroring `edge_cli_no_arguments_runs_all_and_writes_a_parseable_baseline`.
2. [MAJOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:703-732 — `compare_with_baseline` iterates over every parsed row and calls `run_workload(&row.workload, row.size, DEFAULT_BUDGET)` (line 725) with a *fresh* 60s budget each time, while `parse_baseline_markdown` (line 636-663) neither dedupes nor caps rows (it only rejects unknown names). A baseline file containing N repeated known-workload rows therefore makes `rb-bench --compare` run N workloads for up to N×60s (each spawning a detached thread), contradicting the module's stated invariant that `run_all` shares one budget (mod.rs:747-765). — Enforce one shared budget across the comparison loop and reject duplicate/wrong-size rows, or cap the row count. TRACED: parser push at mod.rs:656-661, loop at 723-732, per-row `DEFAULT_BUDGET` at 725.
3. [MINOR] tests/bench_test.rs:650-660 — the write-failure test passes a *directory* (`dir`) as `output`; `timings_path` (tests/bench/mod.rs:617-619) uses `with_file_name("BENCH_TIMINGS.md")`, which replaces the last path component, so `.../rb-bench-write-failure-<pid>-<nanos>` resolves to `.../BENCH_TIMINGS.md` in the parent (normally `/tmp/BENCH_TIMINGS.md`). The timing write escapes the test sandbox, can clobber a real `/tmp/BENCH_TIMINGS.md`, is never cleaned up, and the test can pass because the *timing* write failed rather than the baseline write. — Derive the timing path from `output.parent()` only when `output` is a file, and use an output path whose parent makes the intended write fail (e.g. an unwritable subdirectory inside `dir`).
4. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:644-647 — `parse_baseline_markdown` silently `continue`s on any row whose first cell is not an exact known workload name, despite the doc at 630-635 promising a corrupted baseline cannot compare as if it had fewer workloads. A typo such as `| fibonacii | 20 | 6765 | 1.0 ms |` is dropped and `--compare` exits 0 over the remaining rows. — If a four-cell row carries a parseable numeric size but an unknown workload name, return an error instead of skipping it; also reject duplicate `(workload, size)` rows.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 4
